"Log-Concave Polynomials I: Entropy and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of Matroids (https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.00929)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; Title: Log-Concave Polynomials I: Entropy and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of MatroidsAuthors: Nima Anari, Shayan Oveis Gharan, Cynthia Vinzant ... Based on a lower bound of Azar, Broder, and Frieze [ABF94] this is almost the best possible result assuming oracle access to independent sets of the matroid.\nTitle: Log-Concave Polynomials I: Entropy and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of Matroids\nAuthors: Nima Anari, Shayan Oveis Gharan, Cynthia Vinzant\nDate: Mon Jul  2 23:50:07 2018\n\nWe give a deterministic polynomial time $2^{O(r)}$-approximation algorithm for the number of bases of a given matroid of rank $r$ and the number of common bases of any two matroids of rank $r$. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first nontrivial deterministic approximation algorithm that works for arbitrary matroids. Based on a lower bound of Azar, Broder, and Frieze [ABF94] this is almost the best possible result assuming oracle access to independent sets of the matroid. There are two main ingredients in our result: For the first, we build upon recent results of Adiprasito, Huh, and Katz [AHK15] and Huh and Wang [HW17] on combinatorial hodge theory to derive a connection between matroids and log-concave polynomials. We expect that several new applications in approximation algorithms will be derived from this connection in future. Formally, we prove that the multivariate generating polynomial of the bases of any matroid is log-concave as a function over the positive orthant. For the second ingredient, we develop a general framework for approximate counting in discrete problems, based on convex optimization. The connection goes through subadditivity of the entropy. For matroids, we prove that an approximate superadditivity of the entropy holds by relying on the log-concavity of the corresponding polynomials.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA generalization of permanent inequalities and applications in counting and optimization - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870821000955)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # A generalization of permanent inequalities and applications in counting and optimizationAuthor links open overlay panel Nima Anari ^{a}, Shayan Oveis Gharan ^{b} ^{1} ... We use our theorem to give a new proof of Schrijver's inequality on the number of perfect matchings of a regular bipartite graph, generalize a recent result of Nikolov and Singh [23], and give deterministic polynomial time approximation algorithms for several counting problems.\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: 4 June 2021\n\nArticle: 107657\n\nVolume: Volume 383\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# A generalization of permanent inequalities and applications in counting and optimization\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Nima Anari ^{a}, Shayan Oveis Gharan ^{b} ^{1}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issueView Open Manuscript\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2021.107657\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nA polynomial is real stable if it has no roots in the upper-half complex plane. Gurvits's permanent inequality gives a lower bound on the coefficient of the monomial of a real stable polynomial p with nonnegative coefficients. This fundamental inequality has been used to attack several counting and optimization problems.\n\nHere, we study a more general question: Given a stable multilinear polynomial p with nonnegative coefficients and a set of monomials S, we show that if the polynomial obtained by summing up all monomials in S is real stable, then we can lowerbound the sum of coefficients of monomials of p that are in S. We also prove generalizations of this theorem to (real stable) polynomials that are not multilinear. We use our theorem to give a new proof of Schrijver's inequality on the number of perfect matchings of a regular bipartite graph, generalize a recent result of Nikolov and Singh [23], and give deterministic polynomial time approximation algorithms for several counting problems.\n\n## Keywords\n\nReal stable polynomials\n\n;\n\nPermanent\n\n;\n\nVan der Waerden's conjecture\n\n;\n\nDeterminantal point processes\n\n;\n\nPerfect matching\n\n^{1}--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-Concave Polynomials I: Entropy and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of Matroids - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1807.00929v2)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Log-Concave Polynomials I: Entropy and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of MatroidsNima Anari, Shayan Oveis Gharan, Cynthia Vinzant ... Based on a lower bound of Azar, Broder, and Frieze [ABF94] this is almost the best possible result assuming oracle access to independent sets of the matroid.\n\n## Log-Concave Polynomials I: Entropy and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of Matroids\n\nNima Anari, Shayan Oveis Gharan, Cynthia Vinzant\n\nPublished: 2018-07-02 | Updated: 2018-11-05\n\nComments: Appeared in FOCS 2018\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1215/00127094-2020-0091\n\nconvex optimization hodge theory log-concave matroid polynomial time polynomial\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe give a deterministic polynomial time $2^{O(r)}$-approximation algorithm for the number of bases of a given matroid of rank $r$ and the number of common bases of any two matroids of rank $r$. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first nontrivial deterministic approximation algorithm that works for arbitrary matroids. Based on a lower bound of Azar, Broder, and Frieze [ABF94] this is almost the best possible result assuming oracle access to independent sets of the matroid. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-Concave Polynomials, Entropy, and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of Matroids (https://ieee-focs.org/FOCS-2018-Papers/pdfs/59f035.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.1 years ago; # Log-Concave Polynomials, Entropy, and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of MatroidsNima Anari ... Shayan Oveis Gharan ... Cynthia Vinzant ... **Abstract—**We give a deterministic polynomial time 2^O(r)-approximation algorithm for the number of bases of a given matroid of rank r and the number of common bases of any two matroids of rank r. ... Based on a lower bound of Azar, Broder, and Frieze [7] this is almost the best possible assuming oracle access to independent sets of the matroid.\n2018 IEEE 59th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science\n\n# Log-Concave Polynomials, Entropy, and a Deterministic Approximation Algorithm for Counting Bases of Matroids\n\nNima Anari  \n*Computer Science Department*  \n*Stanford University*  \n*anari@cs.stanford.edu*\n\nShayan Oveis Gharan  \n*Computer Science and Engineering*  \n*University of Washington*  \n*shayan@cs.washington.edu*\n\nCynthia Vinzant  \n*Department of Mathematics*  \n*North Carolina State University*  \n*clvinzan@ncsu.edu*\n\n**Abstract—**We give a deterministic polynomial time 2^O(r)-approximation algorithm for the number of bases of a given matroid of rank r and the number of common bases of any two matroids of rank r. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first nontrivial deterministic approximation algorithm that works for arbitrary matroids. Based on a lower bound of Azar, Broder, and Frieze [7] this is almost the best possible assuming oracle access to independent sets of the matroid.\n\nThere are two main ingredients in our result: For the first, we build upon recent results of Adiprasito, Huh, and Katz [1] and Huh and Wang [23] on combinatorial hodge theory to derive a connection between matroids and log-concave polynomials. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Generalization of Permanent Inequalities and Applications in Counting and Optimization (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.02937)\nciteturn3academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.7 years ago; Title: A Generalization of Permanent Inequalities and Applications in Counting and OptimizationAuthors: Nima Anari, Shayan Oveis Gharan ... We use our theorem to give a new proof of Schrijver's inequality on the number of perfect matchings of a regular bipartite graph, generalize a recent result of Nikolov and Singh, and give deterministic polynomial time approximation algorithms for several counting problems.\nTitle: A Generalization of Permanent Inequalities and Applications in Counting and Optimization\nAuthors: Nima Anari, Shayan Oveis Gharan\nDate: Thu Feb  9 18:46:54 2017\n\nA polynomial $p\\in\\mathbb{R}[z_1,\\dots,z_n]$ is real stable if it has no roots in the upper-half complex plane. Gurvits's permanent inequality gives a lower bound on the coefficient of the $z_1z_2\\dots z_n$ monomial of a real stable polynomial $p$ with nonnegative coefficients. This fundamental inequality has been used to attack several counting and optimization problems. Here, we study a more general question: Given a stable multilinear polynomial $p$ with nonnegative coefficients and a set of monomials $S$, we show that if the polynomial obtained by summing up all monomials in $S$ is real stable, then we can lowerbound the sum of coefficients of monomials of $p$ that are in $S$. We also prove generalizations of this theorem to (real stable) polynomials that are not multilinear. We use our theorem to give a new proof of Schrijver's inequality on the number of perfect matchings of a regular bipartite graph, generalize a recent result of Nikolov and Singh, and give deterministic polynomial time approximation algorithms for several counting problems.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-concave polynomials II: High-dimensional walks and an FPRAS for counting bases of a matroid | Annals of Mathematics (https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2024/199-1/p04)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; # Log-concave polynomials II: High-dimensional walks and an FPRAS for counting bases of a matroidPages 259-299 from Volume 199 (2024), Issue 1 by Nima Anari, Kuikui Liu, Shayan Oveis Gharan, Cynthia Vinzant ... Log-concave Polynomials, Markov chains, high-dimensional expanders, matroids\n# Log-concave polynomials II: High-dimensional walks and an FPRAS for counting bases of a matroid\n\nPages 259-299 from Volume 199 (2024), Issue 1 by Nima Anari, Kuikui Liu, Shayan Oveis Gharan, Cynthia Vinzant\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe design an FPRAS to count the number of bases of any matroid given by an independent set oracle, and to estimate the partition function of the random cluster model of any matroid in the regime where $0\\lt q\\lt 1$. Consequently, we can sample random spanning forests in a graph and estimate the reliability polynomial of any matroid. We also prove the thirty year old conjecture of Mihail and Vazirani that the bases exchange graph of any matroid has edge expansion at least 1.\n\nOur algorithm and proof build on the recent results of Dinur, Kaufman, Mass and Oppenheim who show that a high-dimensional walk on a weighted simplicial complex mixes rapidly if for every link of the complex, the corresponding localized random walk on the 1-skeleton is a strong spectral expander. One of our key observations is that a weighted simplicial complex $X$ is a $0$-local spectral expander if and only if a naturally associated generating polynomial $p_{X}$ is strongly log-concave. More generally, to every pure simplicial complex $X$ with positive weights on its maximal faces, we can associate a multiaffine homogeneous polynomial $p_{X}$ such that the eigenvalues of the localized random walks on $X$ correspond to the eigenvalues of the Hessian of derivatives of $p_{X}$.\n\nKeywords\n\nLog-concave Polynomials, Markov chains, high-dimensional expanders, matroids\n\nMathematical Subject Classification\n\nPrimary 2000: 60J10, 68Q87 Secondary: 68W20, 68W40\n\nDOI\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2024.199.1.4\n\nMR\n\n4681146\n\nzbMATH\n\n7782632\n\nMilestones\n\nReceived: 31 October 2020\nRevised: 13 January 2022\nAccepted: 14 June 2023\nPublished online: 29 December 2023\n\n## Authors\n\nNima Anari\n\nComputer Science Department, Stanford University, Stanford, CA\n\nKuikui Liu\n\nPaul G. Allen School of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Washington, Seattle, WA\n\nCurrent address:\n\nDepartment of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA\n\nShayan Oveis Gharan\n\nPaul G. Allen School of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Washington, Seattle, WA\n\nCynthia Vinzant\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, University of Washington, Seattle, WA--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians 2026 - Volume 6: Invited Lectures (Sections 12–14) | Polynomial Paradigm and Algorithmic Applications (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/25M1826111)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In 2017, we proved a “striking” theorem relating the entropy of an SR distribution to its marginal probability.Theorem 3.1 (Anari, Oveis Gharan unpublished). ... Cynthia Vinzant pointed us to, then recent, work of Adiprasito, Huh, and Katz [2]. ... For any matroid \\(M=([n],{\\cal I})\\) the basis generating polynomial \\(g_M=\\sum_{B\\textit {Base}} \\mathbf{z}^B\\) is completely log-concave on \\(\\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0}^n\\) ... To this date, the above result is the best known approximation to the number of common bases of two matroids; it remains a fascinating open problem whether there exists an efficient \\(1\\pm \\epsilon\\)-multiplicative approximation.\n## 3 Completely log-concave/Lorentzian polynomials.\n\nBecause of the limitations of real stable polynomials, for a long time, we were obsessed with finding a new class of polynomials which extend to all matroids. Such a family could extend much of our understanding on spanning trees and determinantal point processes such as Theorem 2.4 and Theorem 2.16 to all matroids.\n\nIn 2017, we proved a “striking” theorem relating the entropy of an SR distribution to its marginal probability.\n\nTheorem 3.1 (Anari, Oveis Gharan unpublished).\n\nLet \\(\\mu\\) be an SR distribution on \\(2^{[n]}\\). Then,\n\n\\begin{align*} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mu_i\\log \\frac {1}{\\mu_i}\\leq {\\cal H}(\\mu ) \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mu_i\\log \\frac {1}{\\mu_i} + (1-\\mu_i)\\log \\frac {1}{1-\\mu_i},\\end{align*}\n\nwhere \\(\\mu_i\\) is the marginal probability of \\(i\\), \\(\\mathbb{P}_{S\\sim \\mu }[i\\in S]\\).\n\nWe note the right inequality holds for any distribution \\(\\mu\\), and it is tight if \\(\\mu\\) is a product distribution where \\(i\\) appears independently with probability \\(\\mu_i\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-Concave Polynomials and Matroids: Algorithms and Combinatorics | MIT CSAIL (https://www.csail.mit.edu/event/log-concave-polynomials-and-matroids-algorithms-and-combinatorics)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Log-Concave Polynomials and Matroids: Algorithms and Combinatorics ... Nima Anari ... Based on joint work with Kuikui Liu, Shayan Oveis Gharan, and Cynthia Vinzant.\n\n# Log-Concave Polynomials and Matroids: Algorithms and Combinatorics\n\n#### Speaker\n\nNima Anari\n\nStanford University\n\n#### Host\n\nAkshay Degwekar, Pritish Kamath and Govind Ramnarayan\n\nCSAIL MIT\n\n#### October 24 2018\n\n4:00 P - 5:00 P\n\n#### Location\n\nAbstract: I will discuss an analytic property of multivariate polynomials, which we call complete log-concavity, and its surprising uses to attack problems in combinatorics, and algorithmic tasks such as sampling, counting, and inference on discrete distributions. This property defines a large class of discrete distributions that should be thought of as the discrete analog of the well-studied continuous log-concave distributions. Examples of distributions satisfying this property include uniform distributions over bases or independent sets of matroids, determinantal point processes and certain powers of them, the random cluster model and potts model for some regimes of parameters, and several other generalizations.\nI will discuss a recipe for verifying this property and then give an application in which we resolve a combinatorial conjecture of Mason on the ultra-log-concavity of the number of independent sets of varying sizes in matroids. Then I’ll discuss connections to random sampling.\nBased on joint work with Kuikui Liu, Shayan Oveis Gharan, and Cynthia Vinzant.\n\n#### Organizer & Contact\n\nRebecca Yadegar\n\nE \n\nryadegar@csail.mit.edu\n\n#### Part of\n\nAlgorithms & Complexity Seminars 2018-2019--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-concave polynomials, entropy, and a deterministic approximation algorithm for counting bases of matroids - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Log-concave_polynomials%2C_entropy%2C_and_a_deterministic_approximation_algorithm_for_counting_bases_of_matroids)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Log-concave polynomials, entropy, and a deterministic approximation algorithm for counting bases of matroids ... Authors Shayan Oveis Gharan, Nima Anari, Cynthia Vinzant ... This page was built for publication: